      'Hive has a genuinely different orchestration shape. Instead of a compiled workflow graph, it has one execution primitive — the agent loop — and orchestration is itself a tool call. A long-lived queen agent with a role, memory and tools takes a task, does the first piece herself to learn what the job requires, saves the method as a reusable skill, then uses run_worker to fan out parallel clones with narrower tools and strict budgets. run_worker returns immediately so the queen keeps talking while workers run, four at a time by default, and each worker\'s report arrives back in the queen\'s conversation. State lives in a shared SQLite tracker on disk, not in a context window, so the colony can be queried, audited and resumed after a crash. The README\'s worked example is concrete: preparing a Hacker News launch analysis, the queen asks the one clarifying question that changes the answer, proposes a colony for approval, handles PostHog herself, fans out one worker per product, then checks every row and clears an incorrect one before answering. That verification step is the part most multi-agent demos skip. Built-in tools run in-process for shell, files, code search, PDFs, images, web scraping, charts, CSV and image generation, and models come through LiteLLM including local Ollama. The README includes a memory benchmark page and notes that when a whole history fits in context, reading all of it is still more accurate than Hive\'s memory — an unusually honest limitation. Setup is a repository quickstart rather than a pip install at the root, and the model is only part of the work: Hive earns its keep when everything around the model is the hard part.',
    description:
      'Hive is an Apache-2.0 open-source runtime for colonies of AI agents doing real business work. A long-lived queen agent with a role, memory and tools receives the task, completes the first piece herself to learn what the job takes, proposes a colony for user approval, and then hands the rest to parallel worker agents. There is no pre-drawn workflow graph: orchestration is a tool call, and the queen shapes the colony as work unfolds. The single execution primitive is the agent loop; the queen is one loop and each worker is a clone with its own task, a narrower tool set and a strict budget. run_worker fans tasks out and returns immediately so the queen can keep talking, with four workers running at a time by default and the rest queued; each worker\'s report arrives back in the queen\'s conversation. A shared SQLite tracker on disk holds colony state, so results can be queried, audited and recovered after a crash rather than living only in a context window. The README walks through a real run analysing five developer tools\' Hacker News launches, including a clarifying question, approval of the proposed colony, the queen saving a reusable skill after doing the first product herself, fan-out, and a final row-by-row check that clears an incorrectly counted post. Quickstart requires Python 3.11+, Node.js 20+ and git; the quickstart installs uv and ripgrep if missing, offers to install Node, sets up a single Python environment, stores the API key encrypted under ~/.hive, builds the dashboard and opens it at http://127.0.0.1:8787. Model options include API keys from Anthropic, OpenAI, Google Gemini, Groq, Cerebras or OpenRouter, existing coding subscriptions such as Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Kimi Code, MiniMax, Z.AI or Antigravity, Hive LLM, or a local Ollama model with no key. The README warns that the repository is a uv workspace and pip install -e . at its root installs a non-running placeholder, so the quickstart should be used. Built-in in-process tools cover shell commands and background jobs, file editing, code search, PDFs, attachments and images, web scraping, ECharts and Mermaid charts, CSV and image generation. Documentation includes getting started, an architecture overview and a memory benchmark page that notes a whole in-context history is still more accurate than Hive\'s memory when it fits.',
